WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.272
This example uses a cross join with a filter only to demonstrate that joins can multiply rows.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:13.857
In real Spark work, joins can be expensive because Spark may need to move data so matching keys

00:00:13.857 --> 00:00:14.352
are together.

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:20.697
Before joining large datasets, ask whether the join keys are correct, whether the grain is controlled,

00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:24.147
and whether one side is small enough to broadcast.

00:00:24.147 --> 00:00:30.752
Connect this idea to a healthcare analytics platform processing large longitudinal event tables.

00:00:30.752 --> 00:00:36.247
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.247 --> 00:00:43.882
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:43.882 --> 00:00:49.597
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:49.597 --> 00:00:55.478
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
